    name: "Tim O'Brien",
    bio: "Tim O'Brien (born 1946) is an American novelist born in Austin, Minnesota, and raised in Worthington, Minnesota. He studied political science at Macalester College and was drafted into the United States Army in 1968. He served as a foot soldier in the Quang Ngai province of Vietnam, attaining the rank of sergeant, and received a Purple Heart for a shrapnel wound in 1969. After his discharge he pursued graduate study in government at Harvard. His memoir of his Vietnam service, If I Die in a Combat Zone, Box Me Up and Ship Me Home (1973), brought him initial recognition. Going After Cacciato (1978), a novel in which a soldier fantasizes about walking from Vietnam to Paris, won the National Book Award. The Things They Carried (1990), a collection of linked stories about Alpha Company in Vietnam, was a finalist for the Pulitzer Prize and the National Book Critics Circle Award and is now one of the most widely taught books in American high schools and colleges. Subsequent novels — In the Lake of the Woods (1994), Tomcat in Love (1998), July, July (2002) — have maintained his reputation though none has equaled the impact of The Things They Carried. O'Brien is the preeminent American literary writer to have emerged directly from the Vietnam experience, and The Things They Carried is consistently placed alongside All Quiet on the Western Front as one of the defining anti-war texts in literature.",
