    name: 'Franz Kafka',
    bio: "Franz Kafka (1883–1924) was a German-language writer born in Prague (then part of the Austro-Hungarian Empire), into an Ashkenazi Jewish family. He studied law at the German Karl-Ferdinand University in Prague and worked for most of his adult life as an insurance official at the Workers' Accident Insurance Institute for the Kingdom of Bohemia, writing fiction in the evenings. He published relatively little in his lifetime — The Stoker (1913), The Metamorphosis (1915), In the Penal Colony (1919), A Country Doctor (1919) — and left instructions that his unpublished manuscripts should be destroyed after his death. His closest friend, the writer Max Brod, refused to carry out these instructions. Kafka died of tuberculosis in a sanatorium near Vienna in June 1924, at the age of forty. Brod published The Trial (1925), The Castle (1926), and Amerika (also known as The Man Who Disappeared, 1927) from Kafka's manuscripts, in editions he assembled from drafts that Kafka had not finished arranging. Kafka did not live to see his influence or his reputation. The Trial and The Metamorphosis are now among the most widely read and taught works in European literature, and his name has given the language the adjective 'Kafkaesque' — denoting a nightmarish bureaucratic situation from which there is no escape. He is considered one of the most important writers of the twentieth century.",
