    name: 'Vasily Grossman',
    bio: "Vasily Semyonovich Grossman (1905–1964) was a Soviet Russian journalist and novelist, born in Berdychiv, Ukraine, into a secular Jewish family. He studied chemistry in Moscow and worked as a mining engineer before becoming a writer. When Germany invaded the Soviet Union in June 1941, he became a war correspondent for the Red Army newspaper Krasnaya Zvezda, and followed the Soviet forces across the entire Eastern Front — from the siege of Stalingrad to the fall of Berlin. He was among the first journalists to document the Holocaust in detail, writing 'The Hell of Treblinka' in 1944 based on his investigation of the Nazi extermination camp. His mother was among the approximately 30,000 Jewish residents of Berdychiv murdered by the Einsatzgruppen in September 1941; he did not learn of her death until after the war. Grossman worked for more than a decade on Life and Fate, the sequel to his censored novel For a Just Cause (1952), completing it in 1960. When he submitted it for publication, the KGB seized the manuscript — all copies, the ribbon from his typewriter, and even carbon paper. A Soviet official told him the novel could not be published for perhaps two hundred years. A microfilmed copy was smuggled to the West by Andrei Sakharov and Vladimir Voinovich; it was published in Lausanne in 1980, sixteen years after Grossman's death from stomach cancer. Life and Fate is now considered the greatest Russian novel of the twentieth century.",
