    name: 'Samuel Beckett',
    bio: "Samuel Barclay Beckett (1906–1989) was an Irish playwright, novelist, and poet who wrote primarily in French, and one of the key figures in twentieth-century literature. Born near Dublin into a Protestant middle-class family, he studied French and Italian at Trinity College Dublin and lectured briefly at the École Normale Supérieure in Paris, where he met James Joyce and became a member of his circle. He settled permanently in Paris in 1937. During the German occupation of France, he worked for the French Resistance as a courier until his group was betrayed in 1942; he fled to the unoccupied zone and spent the rest of the war in the village of Roussillon, writing the novel Watt in his spare time. His major works were written in the late 1940s and early 1950s: the novels Molloy (1951), Malone Dies (1951), and The Unnamable (1953), and the play Waiting for Godot, written in French between 1948 and 1949 and first performed in Paris in January 1953. Waiting for Godot transformed what theatre could be: Vladimir and Estragon, waiting indefinitely for Godot who never arrives, with no backstory, no plot resolution, and no explanation. The play — which Beckett described as a tragicomedy in two acts — has been performed more than any other play of the twentieth century and established the genre now called the Theatre of the Absurd. He was awarded the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1969.",
