    name: 'Milan Kundera',
    bio: "Milan Kundera (1929–2023) was a Czech-French novelist, playwright, and essayist, and one of the most important European writers of the twentieth century. Born in Brno, Czechoslovakia, he studied music and literature, joined the Communist Party as a young man after the war (was expelled, readmitted, and expelled again), and taught at the Film and Television School of the Academy of Performing Arts in Prague. His early novels — The Joke (1967), Life Is Elsewhere (1973), and The Farewell Waltz (1976) — were published in Czechoslovakia but banned after the Soviet invasion of 1968. His works were prohibited in his homeland for over twenty years. He emigrated to France in 1975 and became a French citizen in 1981, thereafter writing primarily in French. The Book of Laughter and Forgetting (1979) and The Unbearable Lightness of Being (1984), both written in French exile, brought him international fame; the latter was made into a celebrated 1988 film directed by Philip Kaufman. His fiction characteristically blends philosophical meditation, erotic exploration, and historical analysis, often using the particular experience of Central European nations caught between German and Soviet totalitarianism to examine the nature of memory, identity, and kitsch. He is one of the writers most directly in the tradition of Kafka, to whom he devoted extended critical attention in his essays.",
