    bio: "Karl Marlantes (born 1944) is an American author, Vietnam War veteran, and novelist. Born in Seaside, Oregon, he studied at Yale University and then at Oxford University as a Rhodes Scholar. He was commissioned in the United States Marine Corps and served in Vietnam from 1968 to 1969, leading an infantry platoon in some of the most intense combat of the war and earning the Navy Cross, the Bronze Star, two Navy Commendation Medals, two Purple Hearts, and ten air medals. After returning from Vietnam, he spent more than thirty years writing Matterhorn (2010), his first novel — a massive, technically detailed account of a Marine infantry platoon in Vietnam's jungle highlands in 1969, following Lieutenant Waino Mellas and his company through combat operations, racial conflict, army bureaucracy, and the daily experience of patrol in a lethal environment. The novel was rejected by publishers for decades before finally being published in 2010 and becoming a bestseller, praised by critics and veterans alike as the most accurate fictional account of the Vietnam War's ground experience. His second novel, Deep River (2019), is a multigenerational saga set in the Finnish immigrant communities of the Pacific Northwest in the early twentieth century.",
