    name: 'Jean-Paul Sartre',
    bio: "Jean-Paul Sartre (1905–1980) was a French philosopher, playwright, novelist, and literary critic, and the most prominent exponent of existentialism. Born in Paris, he studied philosophy at the École Normale Supérieure and later in Berlin, where he encountered the phenomenology of Edmund Husserl and Martin Heidegger that would shape his philosophical work. He taught philosophy in Le Havre and Paris and was a prisoner of war in Germany for nine months in 1940–1941 before being released on health grounds and returning to Paris, where he continued to write and teach under the occupation. Being and Nothingness (1943), his major philosophical work, argued that existence precedes essence — that human beings have no fixed nature, are radically free, and are therefore absolutely responsible for their choices. Nausea (1938), written before the war, was his first novel: Antoine Roquentin, a historian in a provincial French city, discovers that the existence of things — their bare contingency, their refusal to fit any rational structure — produces in him a physical revulsion he calls nausea. The novel is the most direct fictional expression of Sartre's philosophy and the most widely read twentieth-century French novel after Camus's The Stranger. He was awarded the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1964 but refused it, saying that he did not wish to be institutionalized by it.",
