    name: 'Kate Chopin',
    bio: "Kate Chopin (1850–1904) was an American novelist and short story writer whose work was largely forgotten for decades after her death and is now recognized as a foundational text of American feminist literature. Born Katherine O'Flaherty in St. Louis, Missouri, into a Creole Francophone family, she married Oscar Chopin at nineteen and lived with him in New Orleans and on a cotton plantation in the Louisiana Cane River region — settings that provide the backdrop for much of her fiction. After Oscar's death in 1882, she returned to St. Louis and began writing to support her six children. Her early short stories drew on Louisiana Creole and Cajun culture and were published in national magazines and collected in Bayou Folk (1894) and A Night in Acadie (1897). The Awakening (1899), her second novel, follows Edna Pontellier in 1890s New Orleans as she awakens to her own desires and discovers that they cannot be accommodated within the constraints of her marriage and her society. The novel was received with near-universal condemnation on publication — critics called it 'unhealthy,' 'morbid,' and 'immoral' — and Chopin wrote little afterward until her death from a brain hemorrhage in 1904. The novel was not republished until 1964, when the scholar Per Seyersted rediscovered Chopin's work, and it has since been recognized as a masterpiece of American realism and one of the earliest American novels to explore female consciousness and the politics of marriage.",
