    name: 'Edith Wharton',
    bio: "Edith Wharton (1862–1937) was an American novelist, short story writer, and designer, and the first woman to win the Pulitzer Prize for Fiction. Born Edith Newbold Jones into a wealthy New York family — the origin of the phrase 'keeping up with the Joneses' — she was educated by private tutors and grew up in the closed world of Gilded Age New York society that she would later dissect in her fiction. She married Edward (Teddy) Wharton in 1885; the marriage was unhappy and ended in divorce in 1913. She published her first short story collection in 1899 and her first novel in 1902, but it was The House of Mirth (1905) that established her as a major novelist — the story of Lily Bart, a beautiful and talented woman in Gilded Age New York who cannot bring herself to make the calculating marriage that would secure her position, and is destroyed by the society that claims to support her. Ethan Frome (1911) and The Custom of the Country (1913) followed; The Age of Innocence (1920), set in 1870s New York, won the Pulitzer Prize. She spent most of her adult life in France, where she did extensive relief work during World War One. Her fiction is characterized by the same precision and moral intelligence as Flaubert, whom she considered the greatest of all novelists, and by a detailed understanding of how wealth, status, and social convention shape individual lives.",
