    name: 'Graham Greene',
    bio: "Graham Greene (1904–1991) was an English novelist, playwright, and short story writer whose work explored themes of political turmoil, moral ambiguity, Catholicism, and the contradictions of imperial power. Born in Berkhamsted, Hertfordshire, the son of the school's headmaster, he studied History at Balliol College Oxford and converted to Roman Catholicism in 1926, partly as a result of his relationship with his future wife Vivien Dayrell-Browning. He worked as a film critic and journalist before his novels established him as a major literary figure. His work is often divided into 'entertainments' — thrillers with an explicit genre plot — and novels proper, though in practice the two overlap. The Power and the Glory (1940), The Heart of the Matter (1948), and The End of the Affair (1951) form the core of what is sometimes called his 'Catholic trilogy': novels in which characters wrestle with faith, guilt, adultery, and damnation. The Quiet American (1955), set in 1950s Vietnam and remarkable for its early critique of American interventionism, The Third Man (1950), and Our Man in Havana (1958) are the best of his political thrillers. He traveled extensively throughout his career — to Africa, Southeast Asia, Central and South America, Haiti — and his novels are set across what Greene himself called 'Greeneland': impoverished, politically corrupt postcolonial territories that serve as the backdrop for moral drama.",
