    name: 'Phil Klay',
    bio: "Phil Klay (born 1983) is an American author and Marine Corps veteran whose debut short story collection Redeployment (2014) won the National Book Award for Fiction. Born in Westchester County, New York, he studied English at Dartmouth College and served as a Marine Corps Public Affairs Officer in Iraq's Al Anbar Province from 2007 to 2008. After returning from Iraq, he earned an MFA from Hunter College and began writing the stories collected in Redeployment, which examines the experience of American military service in the Iraq War from multiple perspectives — soldiers in combat, soldiers at home, veterans readjusting, military wives, aid workers, Iraqis — with a moral seriousness and formal range that drew comparisons to Tim O'Brien's The Things They Carried. His second book, Missionaries (2020), is a novel set in Colombia following four characters across multiple decades of conflict — an American aid worker, a veteran, a Colombian paramilitary, and a journalist. He has written extensively on veterans' issues, American military culture, and the ethics of military service, and teaches creative writing at Fairfield University.",
