    name: 'Kingsley Amis',
    bio: "Kingsley Amis (1922–1995) was an English novelist, poet, and critic, and one of the most prolific and celebrated British writers of the postwar period. Born in Clapham, South London, he studied English at St John's College, Oxford, where he met Philip Larkin, who became his closest friend. He served in the Royal Signals during World War Two and returned to Oxford to complete his degree, then took a lectureship at the University of Wales, Swansea, where he wrote Lucky Jim (1954) — his first novel and the work that established the Angry Young Men movement. Lucky Jim, the story of a young lecturer's disastrous year at a provincial university, was an immediate bestseller and won the Somerset Maugham Award in 1955. He published more than twenty novels over the following four decades, ranging from comic social satire (That Uncertain Feeling, Take a Girl Like You, The Old Devils) to crime fiction (The Riverside Villas Murder) to science fiction (The Alteration) to the spy thriller The Anti-Death League. The Old Devils (1986) won the Booker Prize. His later reputation was marred by his increasingly reactionary public positions — he became politically conservative and was famously contemptuous of much contemporary fiction — but his best novels, particularly Lucky Jim and The Old Devils, remain widely read and admired. His son Martin Amis became an equally prominent novelist.",
