    name: 'Hermann Hesse',
    bio: "Hermann Hesse (1877–1962) was a German-Swiss poet, novelist, and painter whose work explored the individual's search for meaning, self-knowledge, and spiritual enlightenment. Born in Calw, Württemberg, into a family of Protestant missionaries with deep roots in both German and Indian culture — his maternal grandfather was the Swiss Indologist Hermann Gundert — he had a difficult youth marked by depression and several failed attempts at formal education, culminating in a brief period in a mental institution. He worked as a bookseller before the success of Peter Camenzind (1904) allowed him to write full-time. Beneath the Wheel (1906), Rosshalde (1914), Demian (1919), Siddhartha (1922), Steppenwolf (1927), Narcissus and Goldmund (1930), and The Glass Bead Game (1943) together form one of the most remarkable bodies of work in twentieth-century European literature — each exploring, in different settings and forms, the tension between the individual spirit and society, between Eastern and Western thought, between the artist and the world. He converted to pacifism during World War One and to Jungian psychoanalysis — he was briefly analyzed by J.B. Lang, a student of Jung — in the same period; both influenced his fiction deeply. He was awarded the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1946. His work was particularly influential during the 1960s counterculture in the United States, where Steppenwolf and Siddhartha became near-canonical texts of the era.",
