    name: 'Oscar Wilde',
    bio: "Oscar Wilde (1854–1900) was an Irish poet, playwright, and prose writer, and one of the most celebrated literary figures of the Victorian era. Born in Dublin into a prominent Anglo-Irish family — his father was a distinguished surgeon and his mother a famous nationalist poet — he studied classics at Trinity College Dublin and Magdalen College Oxford, where he graduated with a double first and won the Newdigate Prize for poetry. He settled in London, where he became the leading figure of the Aesthetic movement and its doctrine of 'art for art's sake,' writing his two books of fairy tales (The Happy Prince and Other Tales, 1888; A House of Pomegranates, 1891), his only novel (The Picture of Dorian Gray, 1890, revised 1891), and the collection of essays Lord Arthur Savile's Crime and Other Stories (1891) before turning primarily to the theatre. His four social comedies — Lady Windermere's Fan (1892), A Woman of No Importance (1893), An Ideal Husband (1895), and The Importance of Being Earnest (1895) — established him as the wittiest playwright in English since Sheridan. In 1895, he sued the Marquess of Queensberry for criminal libel over accusations of homosexuality; the suit backfired, and he was himself prosecuted for 'gross indecency,' convicted, and sentenced to two years' hard labour. He was released in 1897, broken in health and reputation, and died in Paris in 1900 at forty-six. The Picture of Dorian Gray remains one of the most read novels of the fin de siècle and a central text in the literature of aestheticism and the philosophy of hedonism.",
