    name: 'W. Somerset Maugham',
    bio: "William Somerset Maugham (1874–1965) was an English playwright, novelist, and short story writer, and one of the most commercially successful writers of the early twentieth century. Born in Paris — where his father was a lawyer at the British Embassy — he was orphaned at ten and sent to live with an uncle in Whitstable, Kent, an unhappy childhood he later drew on for his partly autobiographical novel Of Human Bondage (1915). He studied medicine at St Thomas's Hospital in London and qualified as a physician in 1897, though he never practiced — the success of his first novel, Liza of Lambeth (1897), led him to pursue writing full-time. He served as a British intelligence agent in Switzerland and Russia during World War One, experience that provided the basis for his spy stories collected as Ashenden, or The British Agent (1928). His novels include Of Human Bondage (1915), The Moon and Sixpence (1919, based loosely on Paul Gauguin's life), The Painted Veil (1925), and The Razor's Edge (1944). His short story collections — particularly those set in the South Seas and Malaya — were hugely popular and influenced the development of the short story form. He lived for much of his later life at the Villa Mauresque on the French Riviera, where he became one of the most famous literary hosts of the century. By his own account he was never a great artist; he described himself as 'in the very first row of the second-raters.' His commercial success was matched by critical condescension that has partly been revised in subsequent decades.",
