    name: 'Arthur Koestler',
    bio: "Arthur Koestler (1905–1983) was a Hungarian-British novelist, journalist, and essayist whose most important work, Darkness at Noon (1940), is one of the defining anti-totalitarian texts of the twentieth century. Born in Budapest into a middle-class Jewish family, he was educated in Vienna and Palestine before becoming a journalist and joining the Communist Party of Germany in 1931 — an affiliation he maintained through extraordinary disillusionment until 1938. He traveled to the Soviet Union in 1932–33 and witnessed the Ukrainian famine. He was captured by Nationalist forces in Spain during the Civil War and held under sentence of death for several months before being released through diplomatic pressure — an experience that shook but did not immediately break his communist convictions. Darkness at Noon, begun in German and first published in an English translation (the German manuscript was lost for decades, only recovered in 2015), drew on the Moscow show trials of 1936–38 to examine why old Bolsheviks confessed to crimes they had not committed. The novel made Koestler one of the most politically significant writers in the English-speaking world. His essay collection The God That Failed (1950), in which he and five other former communists described their disillusionment, was equally influential. His later career encompassed works on science, consciousness, and parapsychology. He died in London in 1983, alongside his wife Cynthia, in a double suicide; Koestler had been diagnosed with Parkinson's disease and leukemia.",
