    name: 'Theodor Fontane',
    bio: "Theodor Fontane (1819–1898) was a German novelist and poet, and the preeminent figure of German literary realism. Born in Neuruppin in the Prussian province of Brandenburg, he trained as a pharmacist and worked in that profession before turning full-time to writing in the 1850s. His early career was devoted to poetry and to journalism — he served as a London correspondent for German newspapers in the 1850s and 1860s, and his dispatches shaped German understanding of English culture for a generation — and to travel writing about the Brandenburg March (Wanderungen durch die Mark Brandenburg, four volumes, 1861–1882). He did not begin his major phase as a novelist until his late fifties, publishing his first significant novel, Vor dem Sturm, in 1878 at the age of fifty-eight. The novels that followed — L'Adultera (1882), Schach von Wuthenow (1882), Irrungen Wirrungen (1888), Frau Jenny Treibel (1892), and Effi Briest (1895) — established him as the central figure of German realistic fiction. Effi Briest, published when he was seventy-five, is universally considered his masterwork: Thomas Mann called it one of the six greatest novels ever written, ranking it alongside Anna Karenina and Madame Bovary. His last completed novel, Der Stechlin (1898), was published the year of his death. Fontane was writing in conscious dialogue with the English and French traditions of realistic fiction — he had read Dickens, Thackeray, and Flaubert closely — while being deeply rooted in the Prussian society he was depicting.",
