    name: 'John Galsworthy',
    bio: "John Galsworthy (1867–1933) was an English novelist and playwright, and the 1932 Nobel Laureate in Literature. Born into a wealthy Surrey family, he was educated at Harrow and New College, Oxford, where he read law, and was briefly called to the bar before abandoning a legal career for writing. His early fiction attracted little attention until The Man of Property (1906), the first volume of what would become The Forsyte Saga, established him as a major novelist. The Forsyte novels — following the wealthy, property-obsessed Forsyte family from the 1880s to the 1920s — made him the most commercially successful serious English novelist of his time. The Saga comprises A Man of Property (1906), In Chancery (1920), and To Let (1921), collected as The Forsyte Saga in 1922, and is continued in a second sequence, A Modern Comedy (1929). His plays, including Strife (1909), Justice (1910), and The Skin Game (1920), were equally successful and influenced legal reform — the Home Secretary H.H. Asquith cited Justice's prison scenes as a direct reason for reforming solitary confinement. Galsworthy was among the most admired writers in the English-speaking world at the time of his death, but his reputation declined sharply after World War Two; he is now most remembered for The Forsyte Saga and its BBC television adaptations (1967 and 2002). The Nobel Committee praised him as 'the distinguished art in the great English tradition of realistic fiction.'",
