    name: 'Laura Esquivel',
    bio: "Laura Esquivel (born 1950) is a Mexican novelist, screenwriter, and politician whose debut novel Like Water for Chocolate (1989) became one of the bestselling Spanish-language novels of the twentieth century and the basis for Alfonso Arau's internationally acclaimed 1992 film, for which Esquivel wrote the screenplay. Born in Mexico City, she studied pedagogy and worked as a kindergarten teacher and children's theater director before turning to writing. Like Water for Chocolate, structured around twelve monthly recipes that serve as both literal cooking instructions and chapters in Tita de la Garza's story, introduced Mexican magical realism to a global readership and was translated into more than thirty languages. The novel's central conceit — that Tita's emotional state while cooking passes directly into her food and then into those who eat it — is among the most inventive in Latin American magical realism. Her subsequent novels, including Law of Love (1995), Swift as Desire (2001), and Malinche (2006), have been well-received in Mexico though none matched the global impact of her debut. She served as a federal deputy in the Mexican Congress from 2012 to 2015, representing Morena. She has been an advocate for indigenous women's rights and environmental protection throughout her public life.",
