    bio: "Sinclair Lewis (1885–1951) was an American novelist and playwright who became the first American to win the Nobel Prize in Literature (1930), awarded for his satirical portraits of American middle-class life. Born in Sauk Centre, Minnesota, he attended Yale University and worked as a journalist and editor before publishing his breakthrough novel Main Street (1920), which satirized the provincial narrowness of the Midwestern small town and was an immediate bestseller. Babbitt (1922), Arrowsmith (1925), Elmer Gantry (1927), and Dodsworth (1929) followed in rapid succession, establishing him as the foremost satirist of American conformity, boosterism, and professional life. He declined the Pulitzer Prize for Arrowsmith (1926) in protest over what he saw as the prize committee's bias toward wholesome American themes. It Can't Happen Here (1935), written in seven months in response to the rise of European fascism, imagined a fascist takeover of the United States and was adapted for the stage by the Federal Theatre Project in simultaneous productions across eighteen American cities. His later novels (Cass Timberlane, Kingsblood Royal, World So Wide) were less successful, and his final years were clouded by alcoholism and declining critical reputation. He died in Rome at sixty-five, having spent his last years in Europe. His Nobel Prize speech, 'The American Fear of Literature,' remains one of the most important documents in American literary history.",
